# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. NEVER commit `.env.local`.
#
# This file has TWO halves, and the difference between them matters:
#
#   1. Everything down to the "Arc Mainnet parameters" block is NEXT_PUBLIC_*,
#      meaning it is inlined into the browser bundle at build time. Never put a
#      secret in that half — nothing there is private. Changing a value requires
#      a restart of `npm run dev` and a rebuild on Vercel; they are not read at
#      runtime.
#   2. The "Indexer / database" block at the bottom is SERVER-ONLY. Those values
#      stay on the server, are read at request time, and DO hold secrets.
#
# The names are the only thing enforcing that split, so read the warning at the
# top of the server-only block before renaming anything.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WalletConnect / Reown project ID  ** REQUIRED FOR MOBILE WALLETS **
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Get one free at https://cloud.reown.com (formerly cloud.walletconnect.com).
# It is a 32-character hex string, e.g. 8f4a1c2d9e6b3a5f7c1d8e2b4a6f9c3e
#
# Leave this BLANK and the wallet picker shows only browser-injected wallets and
# Coinbase Wallet, which are the two that work without a relay. That is a
# deliberate, functional fallback — not a broken state.
#
# Set it and MetaMask, Rainbow, Trust and the WalletConnect QR option appear.
# Mobile users cannot connect MetaMask without this, because MetaMask mobile
# reaches the site through the WalletConnect relay.
#
# The value is validated as 32 hex chars. A placeholder like 'demo' or a partial
# paste is treated as blank, because an invalid ID fails at connect time — the
# wallet button just does nothing — rather than at page load, which makes it
# painful to diagnose.
NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLETCONNECT_PROJECT_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Site logo (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# https URL of your logo image, used in the header and footer. Leave blank to
# render the built-in "A" monogram mark.
#
# Requirements: must be https (http and data: are rejected), and should be a
# square-ish PNG/SVG/WebP at 64px or larger. It is displayed in a fixed box with
# object-fit: contain, so a non-square image is letterboxed rather than
# stretched. A load failure falls back to the monogram automatically.
NEXT_PUBLIC_LOGO_URL=

# Optional: overrides the alt text / wordmark next to the logo.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_NAME=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Footer link overrides (all optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# About / Terms / Privacy default to this app's OWN /about, /terms and /privacy
# pages. Override with another app path (e.g. /legal/terms) or a full https URL
# if you host your own policies. An override that is neither falls back to the
# built-in page rather than rendering a dead link.
NEXT_PUBLIC_LINK_ABOUT=
NEXT_PUBLIC_LINK_TERMS=
NEXT_PUBLIC_LINK_PRIVACY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_LINK_DOCS=
NEXT_PUBLIC_LINK_CONTACT=
NEXT_PUBLIC_LINK_TWITTER=
NEXT_PUBLIC_LINK_DISCORD=

# Native-gas faucet. Distinct from the in-app test-USDC faucet button: this is
# where a wallet with zero native balance goes to get gas. Defaults to Circle's.
NEXT_PUBLIC_LINK_FAUCET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Arc Testnet RPC (optional, but strongly recommended)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# By default the app uses the public endpoint https://rpc.testnet.arc.io. That
# works, but it rate-limits aggressively (HTTP 429, and Cloudflare 1009 if you
# push it), which is what makes the trade-history scan slow on a first load.
# Point these at a private endpoint — Alchemy, Infura, or your own node — and the
# scan finishes in one pass.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ARC_TESTNET_RPC_URL=https://arc-testnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/YOUR_KEY
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ARC_TESTNET_WS_URL=wss://arc-testnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/YOUR_KEY
#
# The public endpoint is ALWAYS kept as an automatic fallback, so if your private
# one is down, over quota or mistyped, the app degrades to the public node
# instead of breaking. Nothing here is required.
#
# Validation: the http value must be `https://` and the websocket value must be
# `wss://`. Plaintext http/ws is rejected — a browser on an https page blocks
# mixed content anyway, so it would fail in production while appearing to work on
# localhost. URLs containing credentials (https://user:pass@host) are rejected
# too. An invalid value silently falls back to the public endpoint.
#
#   ** READ THIS BEFORE PASTING AN API KEY **
#
# This is a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable, so it is compiled into the JavaScript every
# visitor downloads. An API key here is PUBLIC — anyone can open devtools, read
# it, and spend your quota. This is not a flaw in this app; a browser dapp must
# reach an RPC directly, so the key has to be in the bundle.
#
# Therefore, if you use a paid provider:
#   1. Lock the key to your domain in the provider dashboard (Alchemy: app
#      settings -> allowlist your origin). This is the actual protection.
#   2. Set a spend cap.
#   3. Rotate the key if it has been pasted into a chat, ticket or commit.
# If the key must stay genuinely secret, it cannot live here — proxy the RPC
# through a server route instead and point this at your own proxy.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ARC_TESTNET_RPC_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_ARC_TESTNET_WS_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Arc Mainnet parameters — LEAVE BLANK until Circle publishes them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mainnet is enabled only when CHAIN_ID and RPC_URL are both set, the chain ID
# parses to a non-zero number, AND the RPC URL passes the same https validation
# described above. A malformed URL leaves the app testnet-only rather than
# enabling a chain whose transport cannot work.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ARC_MAINNET_CHAIN_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_ARC_MAINNET_RPC_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_ARC_MAINNET_EXPLORER_URL=

# Deployed contract addresses are read from lib/deployments/index.json
# (generated by the contracts deploy script), not from env.

# ===========================================================================
# INDEXER / DATABASE  ** SERVER-ONLY — NEVER RENAME THESE TO NEXT_PUBLIC_* **
# ===========================================================================
# Unlike every variable above, the values in this block are read only on the
# server (route handlers, the indexer, the migration script). They are NOT
# inlined into the browser bundle, and two of them are genuine secrets: a
# database password and a cron token.
#
# Prefixing any of them with NEXT_PUBLIC_ would publish it to every visitor.
# That mistake is silent — the app keeps working, so nothing tells you the
# credential leaked. You can check with:
#   grep -rl "<a distinctive fragment of the value>" .next/static/chunks/
# A match means it is public. Rotate it.
#
# These are read at REQUEST time, not build time, so changing one on Vercel
# takes effect without a redeploy (a running dev server still needs a restart).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL  ** REQUIRED for the chart API and the indexer **
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres connection string for the trade index. This is the one value in this
# block with NO default: a missing database is a hard failure, not a degraded
# mode, so getIndexerConfig() throws when it is absent rather than quietly
# indexing into nowhere.
#
# Absent: every server route that touches the index returns an error and
# `npm run db:migrate` refuses to run. The app itself still builds — nothing is
# read at module load — and the chart falls back to reading logs over RPC if
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CHART_SOURCE allows it (see below).
#
# Use a pooled/serverless-friendly endpoint on Vercel; a serverless function
# gets a fresh connection per cold start, and a direct 5432 connection to a
# small Postgres will exhaust max_connections under any real traffic. Include
# `?sslmode=require` unless the provider already forces TLS.
#
#   DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/dbname?sslmode=require
DATABASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# INDEXER_RPC_URL (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The RPC endpoint the SERVER-SIDE indexer uses to read Buy/Sell logs. Separate
# from NEXT_PUBLIC_ARC_TESTNET_RPC_URL on purpose: this one is never shipped to
# the browser, so a key placed here stays secret and can carry a much higher
# rate limit than anything safe to publish. The indexer is the heaviest RPC
# consumer in the system, so this is the variable worth paying for.
#
# Absent, or invalid: falls back to the public https://rpc.testnet.arc.io. The
# indexer still works, just slower and subject to public rate limits.
#
# Validation matches lib/chains.ts — must be `https://`, and URLs carrying
# embedded credentials (https://user:pass@host) are rejected. Anything else
# falls back to the public endpoint rather than failing.
INDEXER_RPC_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CRON_SECRET (optional, but set it in production)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared secret that authorizes the scheduled indexer run. The cron route
# compares it against the incoming Authorization header.
#
# Absent: getIndexerConfig() reports null and the cron route has no way to tell
# a real scheduler from a stranger. On Vercel, Vercel Cron supplies this header
# for you when the variable is set — so setting it is what turns the endpoint
# from open to authenticated. Leave it blank only for local development.
#
# Generate one with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
CRON_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Indexer tuning (all optional — the defaults are the tested values)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every one of these falls back to its default when blank, unparseable, or out
# of range, so a typo degrades to known-good behaviour instead of breaking the
# indexer. Only set one if you have measured a reason to.
#
# INDEXER_CHAIN_ID — which chain to index. Default 5042002 (Arc testnet). Must
#   match a key in lib/deployments/index.json, or there is no factory address
#   and no startBlock to anchor the sweep to.
INDEXER_CHAIN_ID=
#
# INDEXER_CONFIRMATIONS — how far behind the head to stop indexing. Default 12.
#   Blocks nearer the head can still be reorged, and the index is append-only,
#   so writing them risks recording trades that never happened.
INDEXER_CONFIRMATIONS=
#
# INDEXER_CHUNK_BLOCKS — eth_getLogs window per request. Default 250000. Arc
#   testnet REFUSES ranges at 1048576 blocks (`-32012 requested range too
#   large`), which is not a 429 and is not retried — only asking for less helps.
#   Raising this past the node's cap makes every scan fail; lowering it makes a
#   1.7M-block history cost more requests than one run can afford.
INDEXER_CHUNK_BLOCKS=
#
# INDEXER_STALE_SECONDS — how old the index may be before a normal page request
#   triggers a catch-up sweep. Default 120. Lower means fresher charts and more
#   RPC traffic; higher means cheaper reads and a chart that lags the chain.
INDEXER_STALE_SECONDS=
#
# INDEXER_TRAFFIC_MAX_BLOCKS — ceiling on how many blocks one traffic-triggered
#   catch-up may cover. Default 500000. This is a latency budget: a visitor's
#   request must not be held open while the whole chain backfills.
INDEXER_TRAFFIC_MAX_BLOCKS=
#
# INDEXER_CRON_MAX_BLOCKS — ceiling for a scheduled run. Default 4000000, large
#   enough to backfill the full history from the factory's deploy block, because
#   a cron run has no user waiting on it.
INDEXER_CRON_MAX_BLOCKS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CHART_SOURCE (optional) — the one PUBLIC variable in this block
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chooses where the price chart gets its points. It is deliberately
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ because the browser is what decides, and it holds no secret.
#
#   api   — read the indexed history from this app's chart API. (default)
#   rpc   — skip the API and scan Buy/Sell logs over RPC in the browser, the
#           pre-indexer behaviour. Useful when the database is unavailable.
#   auto  — try the API, fall back to RPC if it errors or returns nothing.
#
# Absent or unrecognised: treated as `api`.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CHART_SOURCE=
